@page "/form-textarea"

<CatHead Title="Text areas">
    The same <code>.form-input</code>, on a <code>&lt;textarea&gt;</code>. <code>rows</code> sets the
    starting height and the class does the rest; the one thing worth deciding is which way the reader
    may drag it.
</CatHead>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.FormTextarea.Textarea" Title="Text area" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>Label, control, hint, exactly as a single-line field.</p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.FormTextarea.Resize" Title="Locking the resize" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <strong>Vertical is the default, not a class.</strong> The browser ships
        <code>resize: both</code>, and a text area dragged wider than its column breaks the row it is
        in and every row below it &mdash; taller never breaks anything.
        <code>.form-input--resize-none</code> and <code>--resize-both</code> are the two departures.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.FormTextarea.InAGrid" Title="Inside a two-up grid" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>Why the default is the safe axis, with something beside it to break.</p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.FormTextarea.Counter" Title="Character budget" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>maxlength</code> stops the typing. The count itself is the app's to render &mdash; there
        is no class for it, because where the number comes from is the app's state.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.FormTextarea.Mention" Title="Naming someone in the text" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <strong>Live &mdash; type <kbd class="kbd">@@</kbd> in it.</strong> Wrap the text area and a
        <a href="/menu"><code>.menu</code></a> of the people it can name in <code>.mention-field</code>, and point
        <code>data-mention</code> at the menu's id. Each item carries the text to write in
        <code>data-mention-value</code>, and <code>data-label</code> for the name it is found by.
    </p>
    <p>
        <kbd class="kbd">@@</kbd> at the start or after a space opens the list under the field, and what is
        typed after it filters the list. The focus stays in the field: <kbd class="kbd">&uarr;</kbd>
        <kbd class="kbd">&darr;</kbd> choose, <kbd class="kbd">Enter</kbd> writes <code>@@alex.fischer</code> in
        place of what was typed and sends <code>input</code>, <kbd class="kbd">Esc</kbd> closes. The list is
        anchored to the field, not the caret. To search a directory, fill the menu from your own search as the
        reader types; the script filters what is there.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.FormTextarea.Invalid" Title="Rejected" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>aria-invalid</code> and <code>.form-error</code> reach a text area unchanged &mdash; see
        <a href="/form-validation">validation</a> for the rule.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.FormTextarea.Sizes" Title="Three sizes" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        The tier moves the type; <code>rows</code> moves the height. They are independent, so a small
        text area can still be six rows tall.
    </p>
</CatExample>
